d in this case is huge and it has not been\nproperly considered by the learned Magistrate while granting bail and hence,\ncancellation of bail granted to the first respondent would render justice.\n3. The learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) appearing for the second\nrespondent reported that though the learned Judicial Magistrate has granted bail to\nthe first respondent, he was not released on bail and he is remanded in judicial\ncustody in another case. It is further stated that some of the accused are still\nabsconding.\n4. I have also gone through the order passed by the learned Magistrate in\nCrl.M.P.No.1623 of 2026 dated 16.06.2026. On careful perusal of the same, it\nreveals that the first respondent was released on default bail and it is a statutory\nbail and the same could not be cancelled on consideration of any merits at this\nstage of the case. Hence, this Court finds no infirmity in the order passed by the\nlearned Magistrate by exercising powers under Section 187(3) of BNSS and this\nCourt finds no merits in this petition.\n5. Accordingly, this Criminal Original Petition is dismissed.\n08-07-2026\nLm\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 3 ---\nTo\n1.The Judicial Magistrate,\nTheni.\n2.The Inspector of Police,\nDistrict Crime Branch Police Station,\nTheni District.\n3.The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court.\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 4 ---\nK.RAJASEKAR, J.\nLm\nCrl.O.P.(MD).No.13429 of 2026\n08.07.2026\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	4	t
