ce?"]	The Court held that: Ruled that where an accused has suffered prolonged custody and trial delay without fault, constitutional liberty under Article 21 takes precedence over statutory bail restrictions under Section 45 PMLA. It was unequivocally established as binding law that statutory prerequisites and constitutional safeguards cannot be circumvented, and non-compliance invalidates the impugned proceedings.	["Objective satisfaction and written reasons recorded prior to arrest under Section 41(1)(b) CrPC", "Mandatory notice of appearance served under Section 41A within statutory timeframe", "Judicial magistrate independent application of mind to police arrest checklist prior to authorizing remand", "Evaluation of triple test: flight risk, evidence tampering, and witness influence"]	Decided	["Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar (2014) 8 SCC 273", "D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal (1997) 1 SCC 416", "Satender Kumar Antil v. CBI (2022) 10 SCC 51"]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	\N	1	f
