s prolonged custody?"]	The Court held that: Three-judge bench held that officers designated under Section 53 NDPS are police officers; therefore, confessional statements recorded under Section 67 cannot be utilized as substantive evidence. It was unequivocally established as binding law that statutory prerequisites and constitutional safeguards cannot be circumvented, and non-compliance invalidates the impugned proceedings.	["Public Prosecutor afforded reasonable opportunity to oppose bail petition", "Court satisfied of reasonable grounds to believe accused is not guilty of commercial quantity offense", "Court satisfied that applicant is not likely to commit any offense while on bail", "Strict compliance with Section 50 regarding right to search before Gazetted Officer or Magistrate", "Continuous chain of custody and seal integrity for seized contraband"]	Decided	["State of Punjab v. Baldev Singh (1999) 6 SCC 172", "Mohd Muslim @ Hussain v. State (NCT of Delhi) 2023 SCC OnLine SC 352", "Tofan Singh v. State of Tamil Nadu (2021) 4 SCC 1"]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	\N	1	f
