Thiruvadi Kumar.A\nFor Respondent : Mr.A.Robinson\nGovernment Advocate (Criminal Side)\n__________\nPage 1 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 o	[]	Principles governing criminal jurisdiction and interim protections.	[]	Petition disposed of in terms of statutory safeguards.	[]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	Crl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nBEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\nRESERVED ON : 30.09.2026\nPRONOUNCED ON: 13.10.2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE S.KARTHIKEYAN\nCRL A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nCNR:{HCMD011313972026}\nAlagar @ Alagesan @ Kedi\n..Appellant/Petitioner/4th accused\nVs\n1. The State of Tamil Nadu rep by\nThe Deputy Superintendent of Police,\nOthakadai Zone,\nMadurai District.\n(Crime No.123/2026)\n2. The State of Tamil Nadu,\nRep by The Inspector of Police,\nOthakkadai Zone,\nMadurai District.\n3. P.Stalin\n..Respondent(s)\nPRAYER: This Criminal Appeal has been filed under Section 14-A(2) of\nSC/ST (PoA) Act, 2015, to call for the records in Crl.M.P.No.418 of 2016 on\nthe file of the learned III Additional District Judge for PCR Cases, Madurai and\nset aside the order dated 09.09.2026 and release the appellant on bail.\nFor Appellant : Thiruvadi Kumar.A\nFor Respondent : Mr.A.Robinson\nGovernment Advocate (Criminal Side)\n__________\nPage 1 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nJUDGMENT\nThis Criminal Appeal has been filed against the order of declining bail by\nthe learned III Additional District and Sessions Judge, Madurai in\nCrl.M.P.No.418 of 2026 in Crime No.123 of 2026 by order dated 09.09.2026.\n2. The learned counsel for the appellant submitted that the appellant was\narrested and remanded to judicial custody on 14.04.2026 in connection with\nCrime No.123 of 2026, pending on the file of the second respondent, for the\nalleged offences punishable under Sections 103, 109, 126(2), 191(2), 191(3),\n296(b) of BNS @ Sections 103, 109, 126(2), 191(2), 191(3), 296(b) of BNS and\nSections 3(1)(r), 3(1)(s), 3(2)(v) of SC/ST Act. Even after the completion of 90\ndays from the date of remand, the respondent police had not filed the charge\nsheet and therefore, the appellant had filed an application seeking statutory bail\nunder Section 187(3) of the BNSS. The co-accused had also filed a similar\npetition. Though the petition filed by the appellant was returned on certain\ngrounds, the petition filed by the co-accused was considered and they were\ngranted statutory bail. Subsequently, the appellant filed a petition seeking\nregular bail before the Trial Court in Crl.M.P.No.329 of 2026, which came to be\ndismissed by the trial Court by order dated 05.08.2026. Aggrieved by the same,\nthe appellant has preferred an appeal before this Court in Crl.A(MD) No.969 of\n2026, in which, the appellant stated that he wants to pursue the remedy before\n__________\nPage 2 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nthe Trial Court seeking for mandatory bail in the light of the decision of the\nHon'ble Apex Court in Sanjay Dutt Vs. State through C.B.I, Bombay, reported\nin (1995) 5 SCC 410. Thereafter, the appellant has re-presented the petition for\nstatutory bail which was came to be dismissed by the Trial Court by the\nimpugned order, dated 09.09.2026 on the ground that after filing of the charge\nsheet, the mandatory bail application is not maintainable.\n3. The learned counsel for the appellant contended that the said course of\naction adopted by the Trial Court is directly against the dictum laid down by the\nHon’ble Apex Court. The learned Counsel for the appellant contended that the\nright to claim the mandatory bail accrues on 60th or 90th day when the\ninvestigation agency failed to file the final report. The said right of the accused\nis an indefeasible right which cannot be taken away subsequently by filing the\nfinal report. The learned counsel for the appellant relied on the decision of the\nHon'ble Apex Court in Rakesh Kumar Paul Vs. State of Assam reported in\n(2017) 15 SCC 67. The learned counsel for the appellant further contended that\nthe date relevant for computation is the date of filing of application and not the\ndate of taking cognizance by the Court. He relied on the judgment of Hon'ble\nApex Court in Sarah Mathew Vs. Institute of Cardio-Vascular Diseases by its\nDirector Dr.K.M.Cherian and others, reported in (2014) 2 SCC 62.\n__________\nPage 3 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\n4. He further contended that no written application is necessary for\nseeking statutory bail and that even an oral application is sufficient. In support\nof the said contention, the learned counsel for the appellant relied upon the\ndecision of the Hon'ble Apex Court in Bikramjit Singh Vs. State of Punjab,\nreported in (2020) 10 SCC 616.\n5. He also relied upon the decision of this Court in K.Muthirul Vs.\nInspector of Police, Samayapuram Police Station, Madurai District, reported\nin 2021 SCC OnLine Mad 17811 to say the indefeasible right to default bail\nunder Section 167(2) Cr.P.C. is an integral part of the right to personal liberty\nguaranteed under Article 21 of the Constitution of India and that the said right\nto bail cannot be suspended even during a pandemic situation.\n6. The learned counsel for the appellant further relied upon the decision\nof this Court in Rahim Vs. State of Tamil Nadu, represented by the Inspector\nof Police, Thalamuthunagar Police Station, Thoothukudi District, in Crl.O.P.\n(MD) No.1517 of 2026, dated 30.03.2026, wherein it was held that even if the\nbail application and the charge sheet were filed on the same day, the\nindefeasible right of the accused to seek statutory bail will not get lost.\nTherefore, according to the learned counsel for the appellant, the Trial Court\nerred in holding that the application for statutory bail is not maintainable.\nHence, he prayed for setting aside the impugned order and for enlarging the\n__________\nPage 4 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nappellant on bail, with or without conditions.\n7. The learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side) submitted that it is\na case of double murder. The appellant has five previous cases. The appellant\nhas already approached this Hon'ble Court in Crl.O.P(MD) No.12570 of 2026,\nwherein this Court has declined to grant bail to the appellant. Further, though\nthe appellant has claimed that he filed an application under Section 183 of the\nBNSS seeking statutory bail, the said application was returned and he has not\npursued the said application thereafter. Subsequent to the return of the said\napplication, the prosecution has filed the final report and therefore, the\nindefeasible right, whatever accrued, is lost. Apart from that, the petitioner has\nagain filed a regular bail application. It was declined by the trial court.\nAggrieved by the said dismissal, the appellant has approached this Court by\nway of appeal in Crl.A(MD) No.969 of 2026 and he withdrew the said\napplication on 24.08.2026.\n8. The learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side) further submitted\nthat though the co-accused were granted bail, they were granted statutory bail\ndue to the non filing of the final report before the court. Now, the right of the\nappellant to file a statutory bail application is lost. Therefore, according to the\nlearned Government Advocate (Criminal Side), the trial court rightly rejected\nthe application as not maintainable. Therefore, he prays for dismissal of the\n__________\nPage 5 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\npresent appeal.\n9. This Court has given its thoughtful consideration to the rival\nsubmissions put forth by either side. On careful perusal of the records, it is\nfound that the appellant herein was arrested on 14.04.2026 in connection with\nCrime No.123 of 2026, pending on the file of the second respondent, for the\noffences punishable under Sections 103, 109, 126(2), 191(2), 191(3), 296(b) of\nBNS @ Sections 103, 109, 126(2), 191(2), 191(3), 296(b) of BNS and Sections\n3(1)(r), 3(1)(s), 3(2)(v) of SC/ST Act. From the submission of the learned\ncounsel for the appellant, it is found that the appellant herein has filed an\napplication before the trial court seeking mandatory bail under Section 187(3)(i)\nof the BNSS and on 17.07.2026, the said application was returned for certain\ndefects. The said defect was not rectified and represented immediately.\nHowever, the co-accused were granted mandatory bail by the trial court.\n10. Thereafter, this appellant has moved for regular bail, it was declined\nby the trial court. Challenging the said order, the appellant has preferred the\nappeal in Criminal Appeal (MD) No.969 of 2026, and the same was\nsubsequently withdrawn on 24.08.2026. Only thereafter, the appellant had re-\npresented the mandatory bail application on 25.08.2026, which was originally\nfiled on 17.07.2026,. In the meantime, the final report was filed on the 92nd day\nfrom 17.07.2026. Therefore, the trial court has held that the application is not\n__________\nPage 6 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nmaintainable.\n11. However, the learned counsel for the appellant contended before this\nCourt that insofar as mandatory bail is concerned, no written application is\nnecessary; even an oral application is sufficient. In this connection, he relied on\nthe decision of the Hon'ble Apex Court in Rakesh Kumar Paul Vs. State of\nAssam reported in (2017) 15 SCC 67. The Hon'ble Apex Court in the said\ndecision has held as follows:\n“Procedure for obtaining default bail\n40. In the present case, it was also argued by learned counsel for the State\n(1996) 1 SCC 722 that the petitioner did not apply for ‘default bail’ on or\nafter 4th January, 2017 till 24th January, 2017 on which date his indefeasible\nright got extinguished on the filing of the charge sheet. Strictly speaking this\nis correct since the petitioner applied for regular bail on 11th January, 2017\nin the Gauhati High Court – he made no specific application for grant of\n‘default bail’. However, the application for regular bail filed by the accused\non 11th January, 2017 did advert to the statutory period for filing a charge\nsheet having expired and that perhaps no charge sheet had in fact being\nfiled. In any event, this issue was argued by learned counsel for the\npetitioner in the High Court and it was considered but not accepted by the\nHigh Court. The High Court did not reject the submission on the ground of\nmaintainability but on merits. Therefore it is not as if the petitioner did not\nmake any application for default bail – such an application was definitely\n__________\nPage 7 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nmade (if not in writing) then at least orally before the High Court. In our\nopinion, in matters of personal liberty, we cannot and should not be too\ntechnical and must lean in favour of personal liberty. Consequently, whether\nthe accused makes a written application for ‘default bail’ or an oral\napplication for ‘default bail’ is of no consequence. The concerned court must\ndeal with such an application by considering the statutory requirements\nnamely, whether the statutory period for filing a charge sheet or challan has\nexpired, whether the charge sheet or challan has been filed and whether the\naccused is prepared to and does furnish bail.”\n12. In view of the above decision of the Hon'ble Apex Court, it is found\nthat in the matter of personal liberty, the Court should not adopt hyper\ntechnicality and it should lean in favour of personal liberty. Therefore, even\nthough the application for default bail was returned, the trial court ought to have\nconsidered that the appellant had applied for statutory bail on the expiry of the\nstatutory period, that is, the 92nd day from the date of his arrest. Only thereafter,\nthe final report was filed before the trial court. Since the appellant had applied\nfor statutory bail even before the filing of the final report, this Court is of the\nconsidered view that the indefeasible right accrued to the appellant on his filing\nof the petition.\n13. Once the indefeasible right is accrued, as submitted by the learned\ncounsel for the appellant, the subsequent filing of the final report will not take\n__________\nPage 8 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\naway the said indefeasible right already accrued to the appellant. The Hon'ble\nApex Court in Bikramjit Singh Vs. State of Punjab, reported in (2020) 10 SCC\n616 has held as follows:\n“29. The question as to whether default bail can be granted once a\ncharge sheet is filed was authoritatively dealt with in a decision of a\nThree Judge Bench of this Court in Uday Mohanlal Acharya v. State\nof Maharashtra (2001) 5 SCC 453. The majority judgment of G.B.\nPattanaik, J. reviewed the decisions of this Court and in particular the\nenigmatic expression “if already not availed of” in Sanjay Dutt\n(supra). The Court then held:\n“13.…The crucial question that arises for consideration,\ntherefore, is what is the true meaning of the expression “if\nalready not availed of”? Does it mean that an accused files\nan application for bail and offers his willingness for being\nreleased on bail or does it mean that a bail order must be\npassed, the accused must furnish the bail and get him\nreleased on bail? In our considered opinion it would be\nmore in consonance with the legislative mandate to hold\nthat an accused must be held to have availed of his\nindefeasible right, the moment he files an application for\nbeing released on bail and offers to abide by the terms and\nconditions of bail. To interpret the expression “availed of”\nto mean actually being released on bail after furnishing the\nnecessary bail required would cause great injustice to the\naccused and would defeat the very purpose of the proviso to\nSection 167(2) of the Criminal Procedure Code and further\nwould make an illegal custody to be legal, inasmuch as after\n__________\nPage 9 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nthe expiry of the stipulated period the Magistrate had no\nfurther jurisdiction to remand and such custody of the\naccused is without any valid order of remand. That apart,\nwhen an accused files an application for bail indicating his\nright to be released as no challan had been filed within the\nspecified period, there is no discretion left in the Magistrate\nand the only thing he is required to find out is whether the\nspecified period under the statute has elapsed or not, and\nwhether a challan has been filed or not. If the expression\n“availed of” is interpreted to mean that the accused must\nfactually be released on bail, then in a given case where the\nMagistrate illegally refuses to pass an order\nnotwithstanding the maximum period stipulated in Section\n167 had expired, and yet no challan had been filed then the\naccused could only move to the higher forum and while the\nmatter remains pending in the higher forum for\nconsideration, if the prosecution files a charge-sheet then\nalso the so-called right accruing to the accused because of\ninaction on the part of the investigating agency would get\nfrustrated. Since the legislature has given its mandate it\nwould be the bounden duty of the court to enforce the same\nand it would not be in the interest of justice to negate the\nsame by interpreting the expression “if not availed of” in a\nmanner which is capable of being abused by the\nprosecution. A two-Judge Bench decision of this Court in\nState of M.P. v. Rustam [1995 Supp (3) SCC 221 : 1995\nSCC (Cri) 830] setting aside the order of grant of bail by\nthe High Court on a conclusion that on the date of the order\nthe prosecution had already submitted a police report and,\n__________\nPage 10 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\ntherefore, the right stood extinguished, in our considered\nopinion, does not express the correct position in law of the\nexpression “if already not availed of”, used by the\nConstitution Bench in Sanjay Dutt [(1994) 5 SCC 410 :\n1994 SCC (Cri) 1433]…In the aforesaid premises, we are of\nthe considered opinion that an accused must be held to have\navailed of his right flowing from the legislative mandate\nengrafted in the proviso to sub-section (2) of Section 167 of\nthe Code if he has filed an application after the expiry of the\nstipulated period alleging that no challan has been filed and\nhe is prepared to offer the bail that is ordered, and it is\nfound as a fact that no challan has been filed within the\nperiod prescribed from the date of the arrest of the accused.\nIn our view, such interpretation would subserve the purpose\nand the object for which the provision in question was\nbrought on to the statute-book. In such a case, therefore,\neven if the application for consideration of an order of being\nreleased on bail is posted before the court after some length\nof time, or even if the Magistrate refuses the application\nerroneously and the accused moves the higher forum for\ngetting a formal order of being released on bail in\nenforcement of his indefeasible right, then filing of challan\nat that stage will not take away the right of the accused.\nPersonal liberty is one of the cherished objects of the Indian\nConstitution and deprivation of the same can only be in\naccordance with law and in conformity with the provisions\nthereof, as stipulated under Article 21 of the Constitution.\nWhen the law provides that the Magistrate could authorise\nthe detention of the accused in custody up to a maximum\n__________\nPage 11 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nperiod as indicated in the proviso to sub-section (2) of\nSection 167, any further detention beyond the period\nwithout filing of a challan by the investigating agency would\nbe a subterfuge and would not be in accordance with law\nand in conformity with the provisions of the Criminal\nProcedure Code, and as such, could be violative of Article\n21 of the Constitution. There is no provision in the Criminal\nProcedure Code authorising detention of an accused in\ncustody after the expiry of the period indicated in proviso to\nsub-section (2) of Section 167 excepting the contingency\nindicated in Explanation I, namely, if the accused does not\nfurnish the bail…But so long as the accused files an\napplication and indicates in the application to offer bail on\nbeing released by appropriate orders of the court then the\nright of the accused on being released on bail cannot be\nfrustrated on the off chance of the Magistrate not being\navailable and the matter not being moved, or that the\nMagistrate erroneously refuses to pass an order and the\nmatter is moved to the higher forum and a challan is filed in\ninterregnum. This is the only way how a balance can be\nstruck between the so-called indefeasible right of the\naccused on failure on the part of the prosecution to file a\nchallan within the specified period and the interest of the\nsociety, at large, in lawfully preventing an accused from\nbeing released on bail on account of inaction on the part of\nthe prosecuting agency. On the aforesaid premises, we\nwould record our conclusions as follows:\n3. On the expiry of the said period of 90 days or 60 days,\nas the case may be, an indefeasible right accrues in favour\n__________\nPage 12 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nof the accused for being released on bail on account of\ndefault by the investigating agency in the completion of the\ninvestigation within the period prescribed and the accused\nis entitled to be released on bail, if he is prepared to and\nfurnishes the bail as directed by the Magistrate.\n6. The expression “if not already availed of” used by this\nCourt in Sanjay Dutt case [(1994) 5 SCC 410 : 1994 SCC\n(Cri) 1433] must be understood to mean when the accused\nfiles an application and is prepared to offer bail on being\ndirected. In other words, on expiry of the period specified\nin para (a) of the proviso to sub-section (2) of Section 167\nif the accused files an application for bail and offers also\nto furnish the bail on being directed, then it has to be held\nthat the accused has availed of his indefeasible right even\nthough the court has not considered the said application\nand has not indicated the terms and conditions of bail, and\nthe accused has not furnished the same.”\n32. Also, in Syed Mohd. Ahmad Kazmi v. State (Govt. of NCT of Delhi)\n(2012) 12 SCC 1, Section 43-D of the UAPA came up for\nconsideration before the Court, in particular the proviso which\nextends the period for investigation beyond 90 days up to a period of\n180 days. An application for default bail had been made on\n17.07.2012, as no charge sheet was filed within a period of 90 days of\nthe appellant’s custody. The charge sheet in the aforesaid case was\nfiled thereafter on 31.07.2012. Despite the fact that this application\nwas not taken up for hearing before the filing of the charge sheet, this\nCourt held that this since an application for default bail had been filed\n__________\nPage 13 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nprior to the filing of the charge sheet the “indefeasible right” spoken\nof earlier had sprung into action, as a result of which default bail had\nto be granted. The Court held:\n“25. Having carefully considered the submissions made on\nbehalf of the respective parties, the relevant provisions of\nlaw and the decision cited, we are unable to accept the\nsubmissions advanced on behalf of the State by the learned\nAdditional Solicitor General Mr Raval. There is no denying\nthe fact that on 17-7-2012, when CR No. 86 of 2012 was\nallowed by the Additional Sessions Judge and the custody of\nthe appellant was held to be illegal and an application\nunder Section 167(2) CrPC was made on behalf of the\nappellant for grant of statutory bail which was listed for\nhearing. Instead of hearing the application, the Chief\nMetropolitan Magistrate adjourned the same till the next\nday when the Public Prosecutor filed an application for\nextension of the period of custody and investigation and on\n20-7-2012 extended the time of investigation and the\ncustody of the appellant for a further period of 90 days with\nretrospective effect from 2-6-2012. Not only is the\nretrospectivity of the order of the Chief Metropolitan\nMagistrate untenable, it could not also defeat the statutory\nright which had accrued to the appellant on the expiry of 90\ndays from the date when the appellant was taken into\ncustody. Such right, as has been commented upon by this\nCourt in Sanjay Dutt [(1994) 5 SCC 410 : 1994 SCC (Cri)\n1433] and the other cases cited by the learned Additional\nSolicitor General, could only be distinguished (sic\nextinguished) once the charge-sheet had been filed in the\n__________\nPage 14 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\ncase and no application has been made prior thereto for\ngrant of statutory bail. It is well-established that if an\naccused does not exercise his right to grant of statutory bail\nbefore the charge-sheet is filed, he loses his right to such\nbenefit once such charge-sheet is filed and can, thereafter,\nonly apply for regular bail.\n26. The circumstances in this case, however, are different in\nthat the appellant had exercised his right to statutory bail on\nthe very same day on which his custody was held to be\nillegal and such an application was left undecided by the\nChief Metropolitan Magistrate till after the application filed\nby the prosecution for extension of time to complete\ninvestigation was taken up and orders were passed\nthereupon.\n27. We are unable to appreciate the procedure adopted by\nthe Chief Metropolitan Magistrate, which has been\nendorsed by the High Court and we are of the view that the\nappellant acquired the right for grant of statutory bail on 17\n7-2012, when his custody was held to be illegal by the\nAdditional Sessions Judge since his application for statutory\nbail was pending at the time when the application for\nextension of time for continuing the investigation was filed\nby the prosecution. In our view, the right of the appellant to\ngrant of statutory bail remained unaffected by the\nsubsequent application and both the Chief Metropolitan\nMagistrate and the High Court erred in holding otherwise.”\n33. In a fairly recent judgment reported as Rakesh Kumar Paul v.\nState of Assam (2017) 15 SCC 67, a Three-Judge Bench of this Court\n__________\nPage 15 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nreferred to the earlier decisions of this Court and went one step\nfurther. It was held by the majority judgment of Madan B. Lokur, J.\nand Deepak Gupta, J. that even an oral application for grant of\ndefault bail would suffice, and so long as such application is made\nbefore the charge sheet is filed by the police, default bail must be\ngranted. This was stated in Lokur, J.’s judgment as follows:\n“37. This Court had occasion to review the entire case law\non the subject in Union of India v. Nirala Yadav [Union of\nIndia v. Nirala Yadav, (2014) 9 SCC 457 : (2014) 5 SCC\n(Cri) 212] . In that decision, reference was made to Uday\nMohanlal Acharya v. State of Maharashtra [Uday Mohanlal\nAcharya v. State of Maharashtra, (2001) 5 SCC 453 : 2001\nSCC (Cri) 760] and the conclusions arrived at in that\ndecision. We are concerned with Conclusion (3) which reads\nas follows: (Nirala Yadav case [Union of India v. Nirala\nYadav, (2014) 9 SCC 457 : (2014) 5 SCC (Cri) 212] , SCC\np. 472, para 24)\n“13. (3) On the expiry of the said period of 90 days or\n60 days, as the case may be, an indefeasible right\naccrues in favour of the accused for being released\non bail on account of default by the investigating\nagency in the completion of the investigation within\nthe period prescribed and the accused is entitled to be\nreleased on bail, if he is prepared to and furnishes the\nbail as directed by the Magistrate.’ (Uday Mohanlal\ncase [Uday Mohanlal Acharya v. State of\nMaharashtra, (2001) 5 SCC 453 : 2001 SCC (Cri)\n760] , SCC p. 473, para 13)”\n__________\nPage 16 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\n38. This Court also dealt with the decision\nrendered in Sanjay Dutt [Sanjay Dutt v. State, (1994)\n5 SCC 410 : 1994 SCC (Cri) 1433] and noted that the\nprinciple laid down by the Constitution Bench is to\nthe effect that if the charge-sheet is not filed and the\nright for “default bail” has ripened into the status of\nindefeasibility, it cannot be frustrated by the\nprosecution on any pretext. The accused can avail his\nliberty by filing an application stating that the\nstatutory period for filing the charge-sheet or challan\nhas expired and the same has not yet been filed and\ntherefore the indefeasible right has accrued in his or\nher favour and further the accused is prepared to\nfurnish the bail bond.\n39. This Court also noted that apart from the\npossibility of the prosecution frustrating the\nindefeasible right, there are occasions when even the\ncourt frustrates the indefeasible right. Reference was\nmade to Mohd. Iqbal Madar Sheikh v. State of\nMaharashtra [Mohd. Iqbal Madar Sheikh v. State of\nMaharashtra, (1996) 1 SCC 722 : 1996 SCC (Cri)\n202] wherein it was observed that some courts keep\nthe application for “default bail” pending for some\ndays so that in the meantime a charge-sheet is\nsubmitted. While such a practice both on the part of\nthe prosecution as well as some courts must be very\nstrongly and vehemently discouraged, we reiterate\nthat no subterfuge should be resorted to, to defeat the\nindefeasible right of the accused for “default bail”\n__________\nPage 17 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nduring the interregnum when the statutory period for\nfiling the charge-sheet or challan expires and the\nsubmission of the charge-sheet or challan in court.\nProcedure for obtaining default bail\n40. In the present case, it was also argued by the\nlearned counsel for the State that the petitioner did\nnot apply for “default bail” on or after 4-1-2017 till\n24-1-2017 on which date his indefeasible right got\nextinguished on the filing of the charge-sheet. Strictly\nspeaking, this is correct since the petitioner applied\nfor regular bail on 11-1-2017 in the Gauhati High\nCourt — he made no specific application for grant of\n“default bail”. However, the application for regular\nbail filed by the accused on 11-1-2017 did advert to\nthe statutory period for filing a charge-sheet having\nexpired and that perhaps no charge-sheet had in fact\nbeing filed. In any event, this issue was argued by the\nlearned counsel for the petitioner in the High Court\nand it was considered but not accepted by the High\nCourt. The High Court did not reject the submission\non the ground of maintainability but on merits.\nTherefore it is not as if the petitioner did not make\nany application for default bail — such an\napplication was definitely made (if not in writing)\nthen at least orally before the High Court. In our\nopinion, in matters of personal liberty, we cannot and\nshould not be too technical and must lean in favour of\npersonal liberty. Consequently, whether the accused\n__________\nPage 18 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nmakes a written application for “default bail” or an\noral application for “default bail” is of no\nconsequence. The court concerned must deal with\nsuch an application by considering the statutory\nrequirements, namely, whether the statutory period\nfor filing a charge sheet or challan has expired,\nwhether the charge-sheet or challan has been filed\nand whether the accused is prepared to and does\nfurnish bail.\n41. We take this view keeping in mind that in\nmatters of personal liberty and Article 21 of the\nConstitution, it is not always advisable to be\nformalistic or technical. The history of the personal\nliberty jurisprudence of this Court and other\nconstitutional courts includes petitions for a writ of\nhabeas corpus and for other writs being entertained\neven on the basis of a letter addressed to the Chief\nJustice or the Court.\nApplication of the law to the petitioner\n45. On 11-1-2017 [Rakesh Kumar Paul v. State of\nAssam, 2017 SCC OnLine Gau 573] when the High\nCourt dismissed the application for bail filed by the\npetitioner, he had an indefeasible right to the grant of\n“default bail” since the statutory period of 60 days\nfor filing a charge-sheet had expired, no charge-sheet\nor challan had been filed against him (it was filed\nonly on 24-1-2017) and the petitioner had orally\napplied for “default bail”. Under these\ncircumstances, the only course open to the High\n__________\nPage 19 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nCourt on 11-1-2017 was to enquire from the\npetitioner whether he was prepared to furnish bail\nand if so then to grant him “default bail” on\nreasonable conditions. Unfortunately, this was\ncompletely overlooked by the High Court.\n46. It was submitted that as of today, a charge-sheet\nhaving been filed against the petitioner, he is not\nentitled to “default bail” but must apply for regular\nbail — the “default bail” chapter being now closed.\nWe cannot agree for the simple reason that we are\nconcerned with the interregnum between 4-1-2017\nand 24-1-2017 when no charge-sheet had been filed,\nduring which period he had availed of his\nindefeasible right of “default bail”. It would have\nbeen another matter altogether if the petitioner had\nnot applied for “default bail” for whatever reason\nduring this interregnum. There could be a situation\n(however rare) where an accused is not prepared to\nbe bailed out perhaps for his personal security since\nhe or she might be facing some threat outside the\ncorrection home or for any other reason. But then in\nsuch an event, the accused voluntarily gives up the\nindefeasible right for default bail and having forfeited\nthat right the accused cannot, after the charge sheet\nor challan has been filed, claim a resuscitation of the\nindefeasible right. But that is not the case insofar as\nthe petitioner is concerned, since he did not give up\nhis indefeasible right for “default bail” during the\ninterregnum between 4-1-2017 and 24-1-2017 as is\n__________\nPage 20 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nevident from the decision of the High Court rendered\non 11-1-2017 [Rakesh Kumar Paul v. State of Assam,\n2017 SCC OnLine Gau 573] . On the contrary, he\nhad availed of his right to “default bail” which could\nnot have been defeated on 11-1-2017 and which we\nare today compelled to acknowledge and enforce.\n47. Consequently, we are of the opinion that the\npetitioner had satisfied all the requirements of\nobtaining “default bail” which is that on 11-1-2017\nhe had put in more than 60 days in custody pending\ninvestigations into an alleged offence not punishable\nwith imprisonment for a minimum period of 10 years,\nno charge-sheet had been filed against him and he\nwas prepared to furnish bail for his release, as such,\nhe ought to have been released by the High Court on\nreasonable terms and conditions of bail.\nConclusion\n49. The petitioner is held entitled to the grant of\n“default bail” on the facts and in the circumstances\nof this case. The trial Judge should release the\npetitioner on “default bail” on such terms and\nconditions as may be reasonable. However, we make\nit clear that this does not prohibit or otherwise\nprevent the arrest or re-arrest of the petitioner on\ncogent grounds in respect of the subject charge and\nupon arrest or re-arrest, the petitioner is entitled to\npetition for grant of regular bail which application\nshould be considered on its own merit. We also make\nit clear that this will not impact on the arrest of the\n__________\nPage 21 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\npetitioner in any other case.”\n14. In the light of the above decisions, it is clear that once the indefeasible\nright accrues in favour of the appellant on filing an application for default bail,\nthe subsequent filing of the charge sheet will not take away that indefeasible\nright.\n15. This issue can be looked into from another aspect also. The\napplication which was returned on 17.07.2026, was re-presented on 25.08.2026.\nOnce the application is accepted and numbered, then it will be dated back to the\ndate of its original presentation. Therefore, for all practical purposes, despite the\napplication was taken on file only on 25.08.2026, it should be construed that the\napplication was filed on 17.07.2026 itself. Therefore, the subsequent filing of\nthe final report after the filing of the application cannot take away the right of\nthe appellant. In this connection, the learned counsel for the appellant relied on\nthe decision of this Court in K.Muthirul Vs. Inspector of Police, Samayapuram\nPolice Station, Madurai District, reported in 2021 SCC OnLine Mad 17811 ,\nand the decision of this Court in Rahim Vs. State of Tamil Nadu, represented\nby the Inspector of Police, Thalamuthunagar Police Station, Thoothukudi\nDistrict, in Crl.O.P.(MD) No.1517 of 2026.\n__________\nPage 22 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\n16. In the light of the above decisions, this Court is of the considered\nview that the appellant had applied for mandatory bail within the statutory limit,\neven before the filing of the final report and therefore, the indefeasible right\naccrued on 17.07.2026 itself. That will not get lost by the subsequent filing of\nthe charge sheet. Therefore, the finding of the trial court that the application is\nnot maintainable is liable to be set aside. Accordingly, the impugned order dated\n09.09.2026 is set aside and the appellant is granted statutory bail on following\nconditions:\ni) The appellant shall execute a bond for a sum of\nRs.20,000/- (Rupees Twenty Thousand only) with two sureties\neach for a like sum to the satisfaction of the learned III\nAdditional District and Sessions Judge for PCR Cases, Madurai.\nii) The appellant and the sureties shall submit a copy of\ntheir Aadhaar Card or any other identity card issued by the\nGovernment in proof of their residence address;\niii) The appellant and the sureties shall file an affidavit of\nundertaking before the respondent police that the appellant will\nnot involve in any offence in future.\niv) The appellant shall report before the trial Court daily at\n10.30 a.m., until further orders, except the days, on which their\nappearance is required before the trial Court.\n__________\nPage 23 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nv) On violation of any of the above conditions by the\nappellant, the respondent police is at liberty to move an\napplication for cancellation of the bail.\n13-10-2026\nIndex: Yes/No\nSpeaking/Non-speaking order\nNeutral Citation: Yes/No\nIndu\nTo\n1. The Deputy Superintendent of Police,\nOthakadai Zone,\nMadurai District.\n2. The Inspector of Police,\nOthakkadai Zone,\nMadurai District.\n3. The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai.\n__________\nPage 24 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\nS.KARTHIKEYAN, J.\nIndu\nCrl.A(MD) No.1131 of 2026\n13-10-2026\n__________\nPage 25 of 25\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	25	t
