t made the following order :-\nThe petitioners / accused 2 and 5, who were\narrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n03.10.2026 for the offence punishable under Section\n310(2) BNS, in Crime No.748 of 2026, 	[]	Principles governing criminal jurisdiction and interim protections.	[]	Petition disposed of in terms of statutory safeguards.	[]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	CRL OP(MD). No.23584 of 2026\nBEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\n( Criminal Jurisdiction )\nDate : 15.10.2026\nPRESENT\nThe Hon`ble Mr.Justice K.MURALI SHANKAR\nCRL OP(MD) NO. 23584 of 2026\n{CNR: HCMD011479652026}\nCase QR\n1.Ranjith @ Ranjithkumar\n2.Poorvika Senthil @ Senthil Pandi\n... Petitioners/\nAccused 2 & 5\nVs\nThe State of Tamil Nadu rep by\nThe Inspector of Police,\nDindigul Taluk Police Station,\nDindigul District.\n(Crime No.748 of 2026)\n... Respondent/\nComplainant\nFor Petitioners: Mr.M.Suresh,\nAdvocate\nFor Respondent : Mr.C.Susikumar,\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side)\nPETITION FOR BAIL Under Section 483 of B.N.S.S.\nPRAYER :-\nFor Bail in Crime No.748 of 2026 on the file of\nthe Respondent Police.\n1/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23584 of 2026\nORDER : The Court made the following order :-\nThe petitioners / accused 2 and 5, who were\narrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n03.10.2026 for the offence punishable under Section\n310(2) BNS, in Crime No.748 of 2026, on the file of\nthe respondent police, seek bail.\n2. The case of the prosecution is that the\ndefacto complainant, who is running a finance\ncompany, and the accused persons were known to each\nother, that the defacto complainant had previously\nlent money to the accused persons whenever they\nsought financial assistance, that on 02.10.2026, at\nabout 07.00 p.m., the first petitioner and the\naccused 1 and 3 entered the finance company, while\nthe second petitioner and the accused 4 and 6 stood\noutside the office. Since the finance company was\nrunning successfully, the accused persons demanded\nmoney from the defacto complainant. When he refused\nto comply with their demand, the first accused\nslapped the defacto complainant and snatched his gold\nornaments and at that time, the accused 4 and 6\n2/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23584 of 2026\nallegedly stood guard outside. On raising an alarm,\nthe defacto complainant came out of the office,\nwhereupon the accused persons fled the scene leaving\nbehind the gold ornaments. Hence, the complaint.\n3. The learned counsel appearing for the\npetitioners would submit that the petitioners are\ninnocent and they have not committed any offence as\nalleged by the prosecution. He would further submit\nthat the sixth accused was granted anticipatory bail\nby the Principal Sessions Court in Crl.M.P.No.2947 of\n2026 on 13.10.2026 and the accused 3 and 4 were\ngranted bail by this Court in Crl.O.P.(MD)No.23514 of\n2026 on 14.10.2026 and that the petitioners are in\njudicial custody from 03.10.2026.\n4. The learned Government Advocate (Criminal\nSide) appearing for the respondent police would\nsubmit that there are totally six accused and the\npetitioners are arrayed as accused 2 and 5 and that\nthe petitioners are having 5 and 6 previous cases\nrespectively.\n3/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23584 of 2026\n5. Considering the nature of the charges\nlevelled against the petitioners and also the facts\nthat the petitioners are in judicial custody from\n03.10.2026 and that co-accused were granted bail by\nthis Court, this Court is inclined to grant bail to\nthe petitioners subject to the following conditions:\n6. Accordingly, the petitioners are ordered to\nbe released on bail on their executing a bond for a\nsum of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand only)\neach with two sureties each for a like sum to the\nsatisfaction of the learned Judicial Magistrate No.1,\nDindigul, and on further conditions that:\n(i)the sureties shall affix their photographs\nand left thumb impression in the surety bond and the\nMagistrate / concerned court may obtain a copy of\ntheir Aadhar card or Bank Pass Book to ensure their\nidentity;\n(ii)the petitioners shall stay at Tiruvannamalai\nand report before the Inspector of Police,\nTiruvannamalai Town Police Station, Tiruvannamalai,\ndaily at 10.30 a.m., for a period of thirty (30) days\nand thereafter, report before the respondent police\n4/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23584 of 2026\ndaily at 10.30 a.m., for a period of fifteen (15)\ndays and thereafter, as and when required for\ninterrogation;\n(iii)the petitioners shall not tamper with the\nevidence or witness either during investigation or\ntrial;\n(iv)the petitioners shall not abscond either\nduring investigation or trial;\n(v)On breach of any of the aforesaid conditions,\nthe learned Magistrate / Trial Court is entitled to\ntake appropriate action against the petitioners in\naccordance with law as if the conditions have been\nimposed and the petitioners are released on bail by\nthe learned Magistrate / Trial Court himself as laid\ndown by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs.\nState of Kerala [(2005)AIR SCW 5560];\n(vi)If the accused thereafter abscond, a fresh\nFIR can be registered under Section 269 BNS, 2023.\n(K M S J)\n15.10.2026\nCSM\nTO\n1. The Judicial Magistrate No.1,\nDindigul.\n5/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23584 of 2026\nK.MURALI SHANKAR,J\nCSM\n2. Do through\nThe Chief Judicial Magistrate,\nDindigul.\n3. The Superintendent,\nDistrict Prison,\nDindigul District.\n4. The Inspector of Police,\nDindigul Taluk Police Station,\nDindigul District.\n5. The Inspector of Police,\nTiruvannamalai Town Police Station,\nTiruvannamalai.\n6. The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai.\nORDER\nIN\nCRL OP(MD) No.23584 of 2026\nDate : 15.10.2026\n6/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	6	t
