 certificate?", "Whether secondary electronic evidence can be admitted without fulfilling statutory conditions of Section 65B?"]	The Court held that: Settled conflicting precedents by ruling that a certificate under Section 65B(4) is an absolute condition precedent for the admissibility of electronic records as secondary evidence in legal proceedings. It was unequivocally established as binding law that statutory prerequisites and constitutional safeguards cannot be circumvented, and non-compliance invalidates the impugned proceedings.	["Section 65B(4) certificate produced at time of filing or leading secondary evidence", "Certificate signed by person occupying responsible official position in relation to device operation", "Device demonstrated to be in lawful custody and regular operational use during relevant period", "Integrity of data output verified with absence of tampering or system malfunction"]	Decided	["Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal (2020) 7 SCC 1", "Anvar P.V. v. P.K. Basheer (2014) 10 SCC 473", "Shafhi Mohammad v. State of Himachal Pradesh (2018) 2 SCC 801"]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	\N	1	f
