?"]	The Court held that: Formulated mandatory directives prohibiting automatic arrests for offences punishable with imprisonment of seven years or less without recording specific reasons under Section 41 CrPC. It was unequivocally established as binding law that statutory prerequisites and constitutional safeguards cannot be circumvented, and non-compliance invalidates the impugned proceedings.	["Objective satisfaction and written reasons recorded prior to arrest under Section 41(1)(b) CrPC", "Mandatory notice of appearance served under Section 41A within statutory timeframe", "Judicial magistrate independent application of mind to police arrest checklist prior to authorizing remand", "Evaluation of triple test: flight risk, evidence tampering, and witness influence"]	Decided	["Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar (2014) 8 SCC 273", "D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal (1997) 1 SCC 416", "Satender Kumar Antil v. CBI (2022) 10 SCC 51"]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	\N	1	f
