re is a strong statutory presumption that cheque was issued in discharge of a legally enforceable debt. It was unequivocally established as binding law that statutory prerequisites and constitutional safeguards cannot be circumvented, and non-compliance invalidates the impugned proceedings.	["Cheque presented to drawee bank within 3 months of validity period", "Return memo issued by bank showing insufficiency of funds or exceeds arrangement", "Demand notice dispatched to drawer within 30 days of receiving dishonour intimation", "Failure by drawer to tender payment within 15 days of notice receipt", "Complaint filed before competent magistrate within one month of cause of action arising"]	Decided	["Rangappa v. Sri Mohan (2010) 11 SCC 441", "Dashrath Rupsingh Rathod v. State of Maharashtra (2014) 9 SCC 129", "MSR Leathers v. S. Palaniappan (2013) 1 SCC 177"]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	\N	1	f
