tal rights?"]	The Court held that: Struck down Section 497 IPC criminalizing adultery as unconstitutional, ruling that treat women as chattel of their husbands violates Articles 14 and 21. It was unequivocally established as binding law that statutory prerequisites and constitutional safeguards cannot be circumvented, and non-compliance invalidates the impugned proceedings.	["Legality: State action authorized by valid and competent law", "Legitimate Aim: Measure serves a pressing and justifiable constitutional purpose", "Proportionality: Rational nexus between means adopted and objective sought with least intrusive impact", "Procedural Due Process: Adherence to principles of natural justice and non-arbitrariness"]	Decided	["K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017) 10 SCC 1", "Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978) 1 SCC 248", "Navtej Singh Johar v. Union of India (2018) 10 SCC 1"]	\N	unverified	Precedential status not checked	\N	1	f
