ding Precedent	IN THE MADRAS HIGH COURT (PRINCIPAL SEAT, CHENNAI)\nAT CHENNAI\n\nCASE NO: C.S. (Comm. Div.) No. 104 of 2026\nCNR NUMBER: MHC010345672026\nDECISION DATE: 2026-03-18\nYEAR: 2026\n\nCORAM / BENCH:\nHon'ble Justice Abdul Quddhose\n\nPARTIES:\nM/s Sundaram Technologies Private Limited\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nAravind InfoTech LLP\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nTrade Marks Act 1999, Commercial Courts Act 2015\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nTrade Marks Act Section 29, Trade Marks Act Section 134, Commercial Courts Act Section 2(1)(c)\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nTrademark Infringement, Passing Off, Deceptive Similarity, Permanent Injunction, Damages\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nPlaintiff is the registered proprietor of trademark 'LEXGUARD' for legal software solutions. Defendant launched software under deceptively similar mark 'LEXGUARDSYS' causing confusion among judicial practitioners.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Whether defendant's use of 'LEXGUARDSYS' constitutes infringement under Section 29(2) of Trade Marks Act 1999?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nApplying the doctrine of imperfect recollection and overall commercial impression as established in Cadila Healthcare.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nAdopting an identical prefix with minor suffix addition for identical cloud legal informatics constitutes actionable trademark infringement and passing off.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCommercial Suit Decreed; Permanent injunction granted restraining defendant from infringing registered trademark 'LEXGUARD'.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice Abdul Quddhose\nMadras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY 