Kant\n\nPARTIES:\nVikas Yadav\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nState of Uttar Pradesh\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nIndian Penal Code, Code of Criminal Procedure 1973, Constitution of India\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nIPC Section 302, CrPC Section 354(3), Article 21\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nDeath Penalty, Rarest of Rare, Commutation, Mitigating Circumstances, Reformation\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nConvict was sentenced to death for multiple murders. High Court confirmed death sentence. Supreme Court examined psychiatric evaluation and jail conduct records.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Whether possibility of reformation was adequately ruled out before imposing capital punishment?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nManoj v. State of M.P. mandates comprehensive psychological assessment and socioeconomic report of convict before capital sentencing.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nDeath sentence cannot be confirmed unless prosecution conclusively proves by cogent material that convict is incapable of reformation.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCriminal Appeal Partly Allowed; Death sentence commuted to 25 years imprisonment without remission.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Chief Justice of India & Justice B.R. Gavai & Justice Surya Kant\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t
