\n\nPARTIES:\nState Bank of India (Lead Financial Creditor)\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nConsortium of Resolution Applicants & Ors.\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nInsolvency and Bankruptcy Code 2016\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nIBC Section 30(4), IBC Section 31, IBC Section 53\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nIBC, Commercial Wisdom, Committee of Creditors, Resolution Plan, NCLAT Jurisdiction\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nNCLAT interfered with the resolution plan approved by 88% majority of Committee of Creditors on grounds of equitable distribution to operational creditors.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Is the commercial wisdom of Committee of Creditors subject to judicial review by NCLT/NCLAT on grounds of equity?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nReaffirming Essar Steel and K. Sashidhar: CoC commercial wisdom is non-justiciable provided statutory requirements of Section 30(2) are satisfied.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nJudicial review of resolution plan approved by Committee of Creditors is limited strictly to Section 30(2) IBC compliance; adjudicating authorities cannot substitute their business judgment.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCivil Appeal Allowed; NCLAT order setting aside CoC approval quashed; Commercial wisdom of CoC upheld.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice Sanjiv Khanna & Justice Dipankar Datta\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t
