\nCORAM / BENCH:\nHon'ble Constitution Bench (5 Judges)\n\nPARTIES:\nAssociation for Democratic Reforms (ADR)\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nUnion of India & Election Commission of India\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nRepresentation of the People Act 1951, Constitution of India\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nRPA Section 33A, Article 19(1)(a), Article 324\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nElectoral Reforms, Voter Right to Information, Asset Disclosure, Article 19(1)(a), Free and Fair Elections\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nPetition seeking direction to ECI to mandate disclosure of virtual digital assets (cryptocurrency, NFTs) and offshore trust holdings by contesting candidates.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Does voter right to information under Article 19(1)(a) encompass disclosure of digital and offshore assets of candidates?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nVoters' right to know candidate credentials flows from freedom of speech and expression under Article 19(1)(a) as held in Union of India v. ADR (2002).\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nRight to informed choice is an integral facet of Article 19(1)(a); candidates must disclose all digital assets, cryptocurrencies, and offshore holdings in Form 26 affidavits.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nWrit Petition Allowed; Mandatory disclosure of candidate digital assets and offshore interests directed.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Constitution Bench (5 Judges)\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t
