Ujjal Bhuyan\n\nPARTIES:\nM/s Green Earth Foundation\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nUnion of India, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change & Anr.\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nEnvironment (Protection) Act 1986, National Green Tribunal Act 2010\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nEPA Section 3, NGT Act Section 14, Article 21\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nEnvironmental Clearance, Ex-post Facto Clearance, Precautionary Principle, Sustainable Development, Polluter Pays\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nChallenge to MoEFCC notifications granting ex-post facto environmental clearance to mining and infrastructure projects constructed in violation of EIA 2006.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Can ex-post facto environmental clearance be granted derogating from the Precautionary Principle under Article 21?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nReaffirming Alembic Pharmaceuticals: Ex-post facto environmental clearance is contrary to environmental jurisprudence and the mandate of EPA 1986.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nEx-post facto environmental clearance is alien to environmental jurisprudence; any project commenced without prior environmental clearance is illegal.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCivil Appeal Allowed; Ex-post facto Environmental Clearances declared invalid; Precautionary Principle enforced.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice Abhay S. Oka & Justice Ujjal Bhuyan\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t
