 2026\nCNR NUMBER: SCIN010056782026\nDECISION DATE: 2026-02-10\nYEAR: 2026\n\nCORAM / BENCH:\nHon'ble Justice Vikram Nath & Justice Prasanna B. Varale\n\nPARTIES:\nRakesh Sharma\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nCentral Bureau of Investigation (CBI)\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nIndian Evidence Act 1872, Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam 2023, Information Technology Act 2000\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nIEA Section 65B, BSA Section 61, IT Act Section 43\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nElectronic Evidence, Section 65B Certificate, Call Detail Records, Secondary Evidence, Arjun Panditrao\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nConviction under Prevention of Corruption Act was solely based on WhatsApp audio recordings and Call Detail Records produced without mandatory Section 65B certificate.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Whether electronic evidence in the form of Call Detail Records can be admitted without certificate under Section 65B(4) IEA / Section 61 BSA?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nArjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal (Constitution Bench): Section 65B(4) certificate is a condition precedent to admissibility of secondary electronic evidence.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nProduction of Section 65B certificate is mandatory condition precedent for admissibility of secondary electronic records; absence of certificate renders evidence inadmissible.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCriminal Appeal Allowed; Conviction set aside for violation of electronic evidence certification under Section 65B of Evidence Act.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice Vikram Nath & Justice Prasanna B. Varale\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	