rime Division, Central Crime Branch, Chennai\n... Respondent(s)\n\nSTATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:\nInformation Technology Act 2000, Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nIT Act Section 66D, IT Act Section 66E, BNS Section 308, BNS Section 318, BNSS Section 482\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nAI Deepfake, Cyber Extortion, Digital Impersonation, Forensic Data Recovery, Custodial Interrogation\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nPetitioner created synthetic AI deepfake video of bank executive to extort commercial cryptocurrency payments.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nWhether anticipatory bail can be granted where digital devices used for generative AI extortion remain unrecovered?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nPre-arrest bail cannot be granted where digital forensics and neural network servers must be seized to trace extortion proceeds.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nAnticipatory Bail Dismissed; AI deepfake extortion ring requires custodial forensic interrogation.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice G. Jayachandran\nMadras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t
