tor of Police, All Women Police Station, Tambaram\n... Respondent(s)\n\nSTATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:\nProtection of Children from Sexual Offences Act 2012, Juvenile Justice Act 2015\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nPOCSO Act Section 4, POCSO Act Section 29, JJ Act Section 94\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nPOCSO, Statutory Presumption, Rebuttal of Presumption, Age Determination, Ossification Test\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nAppellant convicted under POCSO. Victim age was determined solely on radiological ossification with 2-year margin of error.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nHow is statutory presumption under Section 29 POCSO Act to be balanced when age of victim is hovering on border of majority?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nPresumption under Section 29 POCSO Act arises only after prosecution conclusively establishes victim's minority beyond reasonable doubt.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nCriminal Appeal Allowed; Conviction under POCSO Act set aside; Age proof ossification test inconclusive.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice A.D. Jagadish Chandira\nMadras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t
