e, Thoothukudi\n... Respondent(s)\n\nSTATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:\nConstitution of India, Code of Criminal Procedure 1973\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nArticle 21, Article 22, CrPC Section 176(1A)\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nCustodial Torture, Human Rights Violation, D.K. Basu Guidelines, Public Law Remedy, Strict Liability\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nPetitioner's son suffered multiple fractures and renal failure while detained illegally for 3 days in police custody.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nWhether public law compensation under Article 226 is available for custodial torture independent of private civil suit?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nCustodial violence is an assault on human dignity and democracy; constitutional courts have duty to award strict liability compensation under Article 21.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nPetition Allowed; Compensation of Rs. 15 Lakhs awarded for custodial torture and CB-CID inquiry directed.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice G.R. Swaminathan\nMadras High Court (Madurai Bench)\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t
