. Ramesh & Justice M. Nirmal Kumar\n\nPARTIES:\nRameswaram Traditional Fishermen Welfare Association\n... Petitioner(s) / Appellant(s)\nVersus\nUnion of India, Ministry of External Affairs & Government of Tamil Nadu, Fisheries Department\n... Respondent(s)\n\nSTATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:\nConstitution of India, Territorial Waters, Continental Shelf, Exclusive Economic Zone and other Maritime Zones Act 1976\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nArticle 21, Maritime Zones Act Section 6\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nTraditional Fishing Rights, Palk Strait, Maritime Boundary, Livelihood Protection, Diplomatic Retrieval\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nArtisanal fishermen from Rameswaram had 68 mechanized trawlers impounded across International Maritime Boundary Line.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nDoes continuous deprivation of means of maritime livelihood trigger State's constitutional obligation to protect citizens abroad?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nRight to livelihood under Article 21 imposes affirmative obligation on the Union to protect citizens' occupational survival through diplomatic channels.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nWrit Petition Allowed; Central Government directed to expedite diplomatic retrieval of seized mechanized fishing trawlers.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice M.S. Ramesh & Justice M. Nirmal Kumar\nMadras High Court (Madurai Bench)\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t
