. of NCT of Delhi)\n... Respondent(s)\n\nSTATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:\nPrevention of Corruption Act 1988, Indian Evidence Act 1872\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nPC Act Section 7, PC Act Section 13(1)(d), IEA Section 3\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nPrevention of Corruption, Demand and Acceptance, Trap Witness, Circumstantial Proof, Shadow Witness\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nClarification sought on whether demand and acceptance in bribery cases can be inferred purely on shadow witness testimony without complainant.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nWhat standard of circumstantial evidence suffices to prove demand and acceptance when primary complainant is hostile or deceased?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nDemand and acceptance of illegal gratification can be proved by circumstantial evidence, but evidence must lead to sole inference of guilt.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nReview Clarified; Corroboration of demand and acceptance through circumstantial evidence must satisfy chain of proof.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Constitution Bench (5 Judges)\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t
